{"title":"1980 USAMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1980_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Find the maximum possible number of three term arithmetic progressions in a monotone sequence of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> distinct reals.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1980_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider the first few cases for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> with the entire <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> numbers forming an arithmetic sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/8/798966f1e72a24eebf5771150f20ad02897ea691.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(1, 2, 3, \\ldots, n)\\]\" width=\"106\" height=\"18\" />\nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c342b01f0cdef0f23d11a2bc9c5aa67f6d83ccb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 3$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, there will be one ascending triplet (123). Let's only consider the ascending order for now.\nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/1/5218af40079081e694d8b82eeac2d368ef6ea76a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" />, the first 3 numbers give 1 triplet, the addition of the 4 gives one more, for 2 in total.\nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/3/ec37f4048b4f92c2227c9bfe79fcb7d52ab99c9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 5$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, the first 4 numbers give 2 triplets, and the 5th number gives 2 more triplets (135 and 345).\nRepeating a few more times, we can quickly see that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is even, the nth number will give <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/e/94e9e3faa2cd92adccc8deced5fb59574714d0f9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{n}{2} - 1\\]\" width=\"43\" height=\"32\" /> more triplets in addition to all the prior triplets from the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bdca93962a2beaffc172ca394e04fe05bd74ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> numbers. \nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is odd, the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />th number will give <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/5/595d3b6e6b92f60df36c8bc5158d3e196fa48f5b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{n-1}{2}\\]\" width=\"42\" height=\"37\" /> more triplets. \nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/f/eef0203e65664bd00b5f0325c5c5fcd81bcead8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"38\" height=\"20\" /> denote the total number of triplets for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> numbers. The above two statements are summarized as follows: \nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is even, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/3/61358e9c24aa9a4edb6a684d35fbbd55606fce76.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(n) = f(n-1) + \\frac{n}2 - 1\\]\" width=\"194\" height=\"32\" />\nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is odd, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/a/aaaee09e7c4f32d2314afc7c5f7efd099ddfb9bb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(n) = f(n-1) + \\frac{n-1}2\\]\" width=\"193\" height=\"37\" /> \n</p><p>Let's obtain the closed form for when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is even:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/e/55e223845b338be44f7f64aae17c224d0ae3d173.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} f(n) &amp;= f(n-2) + n-2\\\\ f(n) &amp;= f(n-4) + (n-2) + (n-4)\\\\ f(n) &amp;= \\sum_{i=1}^{n/2} n - 2i\\\\ \\Aboxed{f(n\\ \\text{even}) &amp;= \\frac{n^2 - 2n}4} \\end{align*}\" width=\"326\" height=\"164\" />\n</p><p>Now obtain the closed form when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is odd by using the previous result for when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is even:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/9/27921be6de1923f83f054e46b75beff27a78344b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} f(n) &amp;= f(n-1) + \\frac{n-1}2\\\\ f(n) &amp;= \\frac{{(n-1)}^2 - 2(n-1)}4 + \\frac{n-1}2\\\\ \\Aboxed{f(n\\ \\text{odd}) &amp;= \\frac{{(n-1)}^2}4} \\end{align*}\" width=\"321\" height=\"145\" />\n</p><p>Note the ambiguous wording in the question! If the \"arithmetic progression\" is allowed to be a disordered subsequence, then every progression counts twice, both as an ascending progression and as a descending progression.\nDouble the expression to account for the descending versions of each triple, to obtain:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/9/059105a6ded13576ba31ea5183d68508f8b58249.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} f(n\\ \\text{even}) &amp;= \\frac{n^2 - 2n}2\\\\ f(n\\ \\text{odd}) &amp;= \\frac{{(n-1)}^2}2\\\\ \\Aboxed{f(n) &amp;= \\biggl\\lfloor\\frac{(n-1)^2}2\\biggr\\rfloor} \\end{align*}\" width=\"192\" height=\"149\" />\n</p><p>~Lopkiloinm (corrected by integralarefun)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1980_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Unlike in the previous solution, I am interpreting \"arithmetic progressions\" to be in the same direction as the monotone sequence of real numbers. So, for example, if the sequence of reals is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/4/4746705d5fc07cdf77107229c79c1f5162a6a26b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 &lt; 4 &lt; 5 &lt; 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"111\" height=\"15\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/e/a5e022dce42fa7026fc78d111dbaecb6c87fca62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4, 5, 6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" /> counts but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/0/1606505a4a28dfb28fa85ff26fce5b2dabf6bef6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(6, 5, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" /> doesn't.\n</p><p>The first solution proves that there is a sequence with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/3/763f581fc111ccda617738b8f5da7cf8d067e799.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lfloor (n - 1)^2 / 4 \\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"100\" height=\"21\" /> such arithmetic progressions. In this solution, I prove that this is in fact the upper bound.\n</p><p>Count the arithmetic progressions by their center values. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d3dead9cb8d5f6327c737e014850be7a29d0f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> be the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />th greatest number. The number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d3dead9cb8d5f6327c737e014850be7a29d0f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> is the center of at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/2/122d2f188c7ade8abd961fa08564466cd0fad42f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\min(k - 1, n - k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"139\" height=\"20\" /> arithmetic progressions, because any such progression includes a number less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d3dead9cb8d5f6327c737e014850be7a29d0f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> and a number greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d3dead9cb8d5f6327c737e014850be7a29d0f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" />. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/c/efce61aab04f52f5d37708f357df021c8baa7434.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" /> numbers less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d3dead9cb8d5f6327c737e014850be7a29d0f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/9/779ac670ba70c351f02e70dd9cfc0a4991d56b0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n - k$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> numbers greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d3dead9cb8d5f6327c737e014850be7a29d0f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" />, and none of these can be in two separate arithmetic progressions with the same middle term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d3dead9cb8d5f6327c737e014850be7a29d0f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>Thus, the total number of arithmetic progressions is at most\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/5/5351dd6615c9a6b917152cfb1a0eb142e9e4063a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{k = 1}^n \\min(k - 1, n - k).\\]\" width=\"168\" height=\"48\" />\nThis equals\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/3/c63edff111acef77521e60ccd33de40b5840448c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1 + 2 + \\cdots + \\frac{n - 3}{2} + \\frac{n - 1}{2} + \\frac{n - 3}{2} + \\cdots + 2 + 1 = \\frac{(n - 1)^2}{4}\\]\" width=\"483\" height=\"39\" />\nif <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is odd, and\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/e/91e12e1e1633c7e3a42c9edeb172953626d2d50d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1 + 2 + \\cdots + \\frac{n-2}{2} + \\frac n2 + \\frac n2 + \\frac{n-2}{2} + \\cdots + 2 + 1 = \\frac{(n - 1)^2 - 1}{4}\\]\" width=\"521\" height=\"39\" />\nif <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is even. This can be summarized as\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/2/24221820dd5482677ce6259faf7aeff4490fd372.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\left\\lfloor \\frac{(n - 1)^2}{4} \\right\\rfloor.\\]\" width=\"91\" height=\"43\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1980_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1980_USAMO\" title=\"1980 USAMO\">1980 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1980_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"1980 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=1980\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1980_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1980 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1980_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1980 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1980_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1980 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1980_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1980 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1980_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1980 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1980_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1980 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017173415\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.033 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.053 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 275/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1267/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 8581/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   11.879      1 -total\n 56.13%    6.668      1 Template:USAMO_box\n 41.44%    4.923      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:11413:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017173415 and revision id 263338. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}